WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.179
Lesson three point five.

2
00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.622
How we chose which checkpoint to play, and

3
00:00:03.622 --> 00:00:04.963
why the loss couldn't do it.

4
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:10.955
From lesson two point four: Lakh is split

5
00:00:10.955 --> 00:00:12.917
by song, so the validation songs are songs

6
00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:14.050
the model never trains on.

7
00:00:14.312 --> 00:00:16.055
Each epoch, the model learns from the

8
00:00:16.055 --> 00:00:16.709
training songs.

9
00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:18.845
Its weights change step by step, and the

10
00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:20.414
loss averaged over that pass is the

11
00:00:20.414 --> 00:00:21.025
training loss.

12
00:00:21.286 --> 00:00:23.204
Then the weights are frozen, and the same

13
00:00:23.204 --> 00:00:24.643
loss is measured on four hundred

14
00:00:24.643 --> 00:00:26.517
validation songs: the validation loss.

15
00:00:26.778 --> 00:00:28.609
Both go into the run's history, one row

16
00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:29.045
per epoch.

17
00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:32.403
Here's the textbook picture, made up to

18
00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:33.026
show the shape.

19
00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:35.353
Early on, both fall: what the model learns

20
00:00:35.353 --> 00:00:36.933
from the training songs also works on

21
00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:37.930
songs it has never seen.

22
00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:39.094
That's generalisation.

23
00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:41.130
Later, training loss keeps falling while

24
00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.169
validation loss turns up.

25
00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:44.164
The model is learning the training songs'

26
00:00:44.164 --> 00:00:45.701
own quirks, which don't carry over.

27
00:00:45.951 --> 00:00:46.740
That's overfitting.

28
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.569
The bottom of the validation curve is

29
00:00:48.569 --> 00:00:49.608
where you'd usually stop.

30
00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:53.545
Now a real pair, from the run of T one

31
00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:56.164
twenty-eight, the transformer drummer:

32
00:00:56.164 --> 00:00:56.961
twelve epochs.

33
00:00:57.303 --> 00:00:59.865
Training loss, in green, falls from zero

34
00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:02.086
point two zero eight to zero point one

35
00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:02.655
eight two.

36
00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:05.445
Validation loss, in blue, reaches zero

37
00:01:05.445 --> 00:01:08.007
point one nine three three at epoch seven,

38
00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:10.455
with bumps at epochs six and eight, then

39
00:01:10.455 --> 00:01:12.562
stays about zero point one nine four.

40
00:01:12.903 --> 00:01:15.466
No clear turn upward in twelve epochs, but

41
00:01:15.466 --> 00:01:18.028
a gap opens: by epoch eleven, about a

42
00:01:18.028 --> 00:01:20.419
hundredth lower on the songs it trains on.

43
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:22.849
At epoch zero, training sits above

44
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.443
validation, for two reasons.

45
00:01:24.761 --> 00:01:26.939
The training number is averaged over the

46
00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:29.277
whole pass, including its first and worst

47
00:01:29.277 --> 00:01:29.596
steps.

48
00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:32.092
And dropout is on in training and off in

49
00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:34.483
validation, which raises the training loss

50
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:35.280
in every epoch.

51
00:01:35.598 --> 00:01:37.405
How much each adds isn't measured.

52
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:43.093
A checkpoint is a saved copy of the model

53
00:01:43.093 --> 00:01:45.736
at the end of an epoch: every weight, plus

54
00:01:45.736 --> 00:01:47.903
the optimiser's state, so training can

55
00:01:47.903 --> 00:01:48.802
carry on from it.

56
00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:51.286
By default the trainer keeps one file,

57
00:01:51.286 --> 00:01:53.242
overwritten whenever validation loss

58
00:01:53.242 --> 00:01:54.193
reaches a new low.

59
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.730
With the flag save every epoch, it also

60
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.686
keeps every epoch under its own name.

61
00:01:59.165 --> 00:02:01.191
For T one twenty-eight that's twelve

62
00:02:01.191 --> 00:02:03.766
files, epoch zero to eleven, each with its

63
00:02:03.766 --> 00:02:04.642
validation loss.

64
00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:07.326
The usual rule is to play the one with the

65
00:02:07.326 --> 00:02:09.571
lowest validation loss: epoch seven.

66
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.474
The question for the rest of this lesson:

67
00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:14.446
is the lowest loss the best drummer?

68
00:02:18.007 --> 00:02:19.985
On the thirteenth of September, one LSTM

69
00:02:19.985 --> 00:02:21.918
run kept all twelve of its checkpoints,

70
00:02:21.918 --> 00:02:23.436
and the battery scored every one.

71
00:02:23.712 --> 00:02:25.921
Here's its validation loss: mostly down,

72
00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:27.808
by a few hundredths, with one spike at

73
00:02:27.808 --> 00:02:28.268
epoch six.

74
00:02:28.544 --> 00:02:30.569
And here's the battery's following score,

75
00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:32.455
follow minus leak from lesson five point

76
00:02:32.455 --> 00:02:34.250
six, on two hundred and ninety-seven

77
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.032
validation songs.

78
00:02:35.308 --> 00:02:37.241
The green line is the songs' own drummers.

79
00:02:37.602 --> 00:02:40.078
Between neighbouring epochs it swings:

80
00:02:40.078 --> 00:02:42.553
plus zero point two one two at epoch two,

81
00:02:42.553 --> 00:02:44.804
minus zero point zero two five at epoch

82
00:02:44.804 --> 00:02:47.167
three, plus zero point two eight two at

83
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:47.786
epoch four.

84
00:02:48.124 --> 00:02:50.487
At epochs three, five and six the model

85
00:02:50.487 --> 00:02:53.188
collapsed to a near-static beat: its drums

86
00:02:53.188 --> 00:02:55.044
barely moved when the guitar did.

87
00:02:55.522 --> 00:02:56.700
The loss didn't notice.

88
00:02:57.007 --> 00:02:59.413
Epoch three's loss was the lowest so far:

89
00:02:59.413 --> 00:03:01.563
stop the run anywhere from epoch three to

90
00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:03.765
six, and the lowest-loss checkpoint is a

91
00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:04.533
collapsed beat.

92
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.246
That's the epoch lottery: neighbours with

93
00:03:07.246 --> 00:03:08.833
nearly the same loss play very

94
00:03:08.833 --> 00:03:11.035
differently, so a pick by loss is a draw.

95
00:03:11.509 --> 00:03:13.866
Here, the lowest loss at the end, epoch

96
00:03:13.866 --> 00:03:16.061
ten, happened to be the battery's best

97
00:03:16.061 --> 00:03:16.275
too.

98
00:03:16.597 --> 00:03:17.346
That was luck.

99
00:03:17.668 --> 00:03:19.863
In another run of the same round, with

100
00:03:19.863 --> 00:03:22.059
input dropout, the loss's epoch scored

101
00:03:22.059 --> 00:03:24.254
plus zero point one nine four, and the

102
00:03:24.254 --> 00:03:26.664
battery's best, plus zero point four three

103
00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:26.878
six.

104
00:03:30.417 --> 00:03:32.538
Recall the loss from lesson three point

105
00:03:32.538 --> 00:03:32.750
two.

106
00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:35.349
For each slot t and drum v, a penalty,

107
00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.524
ell, for how wrong the probability was.

108
00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:40.123
Summed over the nine drums, kept only on

109
00:03:40.123 --> 00:03:42.351
real slots by the mask, m, added over

110
00:03:42.351 --> 00:03:44.738
every slot, and divided by nine times N,

111
00:03:44.738 --> 00:03:46.064
the number of real slots.

112
00:03:46.382 --> 00:03:48.238
So every real slot counts the same.

113
00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:50.311
The battery's following is measured where

114
00:03:50.311 --> 00:03:51.787
the guitar changes section, against

115
00:03:51.787 --> 00:03:52.408
everywhere else.

116
00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:54.389
On the two hundred and ninety-seven songs,

117
00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:55.982
one thousand two hundred and eighty-four

118
00:03:55.982 --> 00:03:57.458
of sixteen thousand eight hundred and

119
00:03:57.458 --> 00:03:58.895
eighty-seven window changes are at a

120
00:03:58.895 --> 00:04:00.760
boundary: seven point six percent, about

121
00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:01.381
one in thirteen.

122
00:04:01.614 --> 00:04:03.284
The rest is routine: the groove going

123
00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:03.517
round.

124
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.265
A model that plays the groove well has

125
00:04:05.265 --> 00:04:06.974
earned nearly all of its loss, whether or

126
00:04:06.974 --> 00:04:08.256
not it changes at the boundaries.

127
00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:10.260
So the loss can't see following.

128
00:04:10.574 --> 00:04:12.880
In the lottery, collapsed epoch three had

129
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.871
a lower loss than epoch four, which

130
00:04:14.871 --> 00:04:15.919
followed the guitar.

131
00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.094
In round two, and again in the

132
00:04:19.094 --> 00:04:21.294
architecture run, the battery scored every

133
00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.664
epoch and a rule picked one.

134
00:04:22.957 --> 00:04:24.913
A kit gate keeps only epochs whose kick

135
00:04:24.913 --> 00:04:26.918
and snare play at least half as often as

136
00:04:26.918 --> 00:04:29.217
the real drummer's: two runs' top-scoring

137
00:04:29.217 --> 00:04:30.586
epochs had played hats only.

138
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.640
Then the best following among those.

139
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:35.134
The loss's epoch is printed beside it, for

140
00:04:35.134 --> 00:04:35.917
comparison only.

141
00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.745
One more thing sits inside every score:

142
00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:40.899
the decoder, which turns probabilities

143
00:04:40.899 --> 00:04:41.425
into hits.

144
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.737
When it was changed, the ranking of

145
00:04:43.737 --> 00:04:45.997
checkpoints changed too, so it's part of

146
00:04:45.997 --> 00:04:46.733
what's scored.

147
00:04:47.048 --> 00:04:48.625
That's lesson four point nine.

148
00:04:50.245 --> 00:04:52.527
Back to T one twenty-eight, one of the

149
00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:53.975
drummers in the live link.

150
00:04:54.309 --> 00:04:56.369
The battery scored all twelve of its

151
00:04:56.369 --> 00:04:56.758
epochs.

152
00:04:57.092 --> 00:04:59.431
Its lowest validation loss is epoch seven.

153
00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:02.047
The battery's pre-registered rule picked

154
00:05:02.047 --> 00:05:02.604
epoch ten.

155
00:05:02.938 --> 00:05:05.221
The one in the live link is epoch eleven.

156
00:05:05.715 --> 00:05:06.299
Why eleven?

157
00:05:06.617 --> 00:05:08.845
The day before, blind listening had put

158
00:05:08.845 --> 00:05:11.231
the rule's picks at or near the bottom, so

159
00:05:11.231 --> 00:05:13.141
the rule was set aside for choosing.

160
00:05:13.459 --> 00:05:15.687
The team's listening deck took each run's

161
00:05:15.687 --> 00:05:17.755
last epoch, once the battery's gates

162
00:05:17.755 --> 00:05:20.089
showed it hadn't collapsed: only seven

163
00:05:20.089 --> 00:05:22.210
percent of its thirty-two-bar stretches

164
00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:23.536
lost their kick or snare.

165
00:05:23.855 --> 00:05:26.241
Epoch eleven passed the gates but one, the

166
00:05:26.241 --> 00:05:28.522
follow floor, missed by four thousandths.

167
00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.962
Then Jaime rated it five out of five on

168
00:05:30.962 --> 00:05:31.757
all four songs.

169
00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:34.303
Every drummer in the live link but one is

170
00:05:34.303 --> 00:05:36.319
its run's last epoch, like this one.

171
00:05:36.637 --> 00:05:39.077
The exception is the old LSTM favourite:

172
00:05:39.077 --> 00:05:40.827
its run's lowest-loss checkpoint.

173
00:05:41.262 --> 00:05:44.027
Across these three, the loss moves by five

174
00:05:44.027 --> 00:05:45.010
ten-thousandths.

175
00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:47.775
Follow minus leak moves from plus zero

176
00:05:47.775 --> 00:05:50.294
point zero nine three to plus zero point

177
00:05:50.294 --> 00:05:51.216
one four eight.

178
00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:52.813
The loss had no say.

179
00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:57.408
One more catch.

180
00:05:57.655 --> 00:05:59.427
Imagine twelve epochs that are all equally

181
00:05:59.427 --> 00:06:01.239
good, with a true score of zero point one

182
00:06:01.239 --> 00:06:03.093
zero, each measured on the same songs with

183
00:06:03.093 --> 00:06:03.711
a little noise.

184
00:06:03.958 --> 00:06:05.029
These numbers are made up.

185
00:06:05.276 --> 00:06:06.883
Pick the best, and you've picked the

186
00:06:06.883 --> 00:06:08.737
luckiest draw: zero point one four nine.

187
00:06:08.984 --> 00:06:10.632
Score it again on fresh songs, and on

188
00:06:10.632 --> 00:06:12.362
average it lands back near zero point one

189
00:06:12.362 --> 00:06:12.568
zero.

190
00:06:12.815 --> 00:06:14.669
That's selection bias: the best of many

191
00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:16.564
tries, scored on the songs that chose it,

192
00:06:16.564 --> 00:06:17.553
looks better than it is.

193
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.484
Our numbers carry this bias.

194
00:06:20.802 --> 00:06:22.869
The rule's picks in the results tables

195
00:06:22.869 --> 00:06:24.990
were chosen on the same two hundred and

196
00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:27.375
ninety-seven songs they're then scored on,

197
00:06:27.375 --> 00:06:29.601
so those scores are optimistic, and the

198
00:06:29.601 --> 00:06:31.668
confidence intervals leave the picking

199
00:06:31.668 --> 00:06:31.880
out.

200
00:06:32.198 --> 00:06:34.371
The epoch elevens we play weren't picked

201
00:06:34.371 --> 00:06:36.969
by score, so for them the bias is weaker:

202
00:06:36.969 --> 00:06:38.241
only the gate screening.

203
00:06:38.559 --> 00:06:40.785
The fix is to re-score the final picks on

204
00:06:40.785 --> 00:06:42.799
songs that played no part in choosing

205
00:06:42.799 --> 00:06:45.078
them, or to state the bias in the report.

206
00:06:45.396 --> 00:06:47.676
As of the sixteenth of October that hasn't

207
00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:50.220
been done: the newest scores still use the

208
00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.492
same four hundred songs.

209
00:06:53.161 --> 00:06:55.445
The loss trains the model, but it can't

210
00:06:55.445 --> 00:06:56.424
rank the drummers.

211
00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.925
One honest footnote: the ear's old

212
00:06:58.925 --> 00:07:01.317
favourite, the LSTM anchor, is itself a

213
00:07:01.317 --> 00:07:02.568
lowest-loss checkpoint.

214
00:07:02.894 --> 00:07:05.450
A loss pick can sound good; it just can't

215
00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:06.211
be trusted to.

216
00:07:06.537 --> 00:07:09.093
Keep every epoch, score every one, gate,

217
00:07:09.093 --> 00:07:09.745
then listen.

218
00:07:10.072 --> 00:07:12.192
And a picked score is optimistic until

219
00:07:12.192 --> 00:07:13.823
it's re-scored on fresh songs.

220
00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.488
This lesson pairs with row six of the year

221
00:07:16.488 --> 00:07:18.826
explainer, and the Reading the code part

222
00:07:18.826 --> 00:07:20.131
of the course explainer.

223
00:07:20.457 --> 00:07:22.795
Next, topic four: the architectures.

